/mnt/zer0models/z0-wt/salvage/z0evals-optchat-post/.review/round2-edit-2.py
diff --git a/.review/round2-edit-2.py b/.review/round2-edit-2.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..c3913a43468a649f4ded1bdc51483e521fde54a0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.review/round2-edit-2.py
@@ -0,0 +1,328 @@
+import hashlib
+import json
+import re
+from pathlib import Path
+
+ROOT = Path.cwd()
+POST = ROOT / 'posts/optchat-claude-code-v0.md'
+SUMMARY = ROOT / 'studies/optchat-claude-code-v0/summary.json'
+MANIFEST = ROOT / 'studies/optchat-claude-code-v0/manifest.yaml'
+RAW = ROOT.parent / 'optchat-trial/results'
+before = POST.read_text()
+post = before
+summary = json.loads(SUMMARY.read_text())
+summary_before = json.loads(SUMMARY.read_text())
+
+
+def replace(old, new):
+    global post
+    assert post.count(old) == 1, (old, post.count(old))
+    post = post.replace(old, new)
+
+
+def paragraph(text, needle):
+    candidates = [p for p in text.split('\n\n') if needle in p]
+    assert len(candidates) == 1, (needle, len(candidates))
+    return candidates[0].strip()
+
+
+replace('title: "uniichat through claude code: a measured crossover and an untested memory claim"',
+        'title: "uniichat through claude code: a price-dependent crossover and an untested memory benefit"')
+replace('subtitle: "two rounds of the endless-chat trial: cheaper turns over a longer replay, a cheaper compactor, and failures worth asking about."',
+        'subtitle: "112 turns, three real batches, and a total-cost result that depends on cache-read and compactor prices."')
+replace('  - id: corrections\n    label: what we got wrong in round 1\n', '')
+replace('  - id: limits\n', '  - id: corrections\n    label: corrections to the short trial\n  - id: limits\n')
+lede = paragraph(post, '<div class="lede">')
+replace(lede, '''<div class="lede">
+We rebuilt Victor Taelin's UniiChat from the saved gist and ran it beside a continued Claude Code session for 112 tool-heavy Sonnet turns, logging 1,161 messages and three real view-reduction batches. Turn calls became cumulatively cheaper from turn 57 in weighted tokens and turn 80 in CLI USD estimates. Including Haiku-low compactions, the weighted-token crossover was turn 82 only under the stated combination of a 0.1-times-input cache-read weight and Haiku priced at one twentieth of Sonnet. At one third, total cost never crossed within the run; in the CLI's USD estimates it also had not crossed at turn 112, 6.019 versus 5.995. The continued session had a 1,000,000-token window, never auto-compacted and answered 54/54 recall questions, so the memory benefit remains untested. We want the author's help interpreting the price assumptions, shared-prompt replies and cache behaviour; six questions follow the evidence.
+</div>''')
+
+start = post.index('The specification addresses sessions')
+end = post.index('Round 1 tested line writing', start)
+replace(post[start:end], '''The specification addresses sessions that fill up, get compacted or dropped, and lose continuity. Its remedy is a permanent verbatim log, a binary tree of summaries and a saved view growing from 64 to 128 KB before a batch reduces it. Recent messages have fine summaries; older messages have coarse ones. Fresh turns use `zoom` to open summaries until they reach the original message. The log preserves the words; successful recall also requires the agent to notice the relevant summary and retrieve it. That is the distinction behind our test of "nothing is ever lost."
+
+The gist reports two simulations. Batching merges costs about 4 times less than merging at every message, 21 versus 80 line-inputs per message over 30,000 messages. Replaying 3,000 messages against a cache model gave cache-read shares of 98.6% of the prefix for turns and 96.2% for compactions. These are source-reported simulation results. The gist makes no cost comparison with a continued session at any length. That baseline is ours.
+
+Our rebuild preserves the append-only log, summary tree, saved view, last-message merge ordering, batch schedule, ruler and retry text. It uses the gist's shared system prompt, omitting the device paragraph as permitted, and supplies `zoom` and `date`. Delivery into an active call, images, paged long messages, subagents, zoom by name and device state were outside the exercised implementation. We evaluated this rebuild through Claude Code on a subscription login; Taelin's own deployment was not tested.
+
+''')
+replace('These are measured programme counts, not independent samples.', 'Counts are measured requests for the programme, including retries.')
+replace('| round 2, E4 |', '| round 2, compactor sweep |')
+replace('| round 2, E5 |', '| round 2, longer replay |')
+replace('| round 2, E6 |', '| round 2, overhead probes |')
+
+# Relocate and shorten the old-account discussion for an author seeing this study first.
+start = post.index('## what we got wrong in round 1')
+end = post.index('## the measured crossover', start)
+old_corrections = post[start:end]
+replace(old_corrections, '')
+short_corrections = '''## corrections to the short trial {#corrections}
+
+Our earlier account overstated constraints and generalized from an expensive compactor. Cache lifetime is configurable, as the warm probes above establish. The gist asks for a cheap model and says UniiChat uses Haiku at xhigh; that choice warranted testing, but its cost is not unavoidable. The low-effort replay changes both the workload and the compactor setting.
+
+The round-1 line-writing analysis summed the CLI's cumulative process USD at every retry, counting earlier spend again. No USD figure from that line-writing experiment was published here. The same defect slightly affected the short paired-chat totals. Differenced estimates are USD 0.635 and 0.461 versus source-reported 0.640 and 0.468, n=26 turns per arm. The Sonnet whole-arm ratio stays 5.0 times; Haiku changes from 58 times to about 57. In the new low-effort sweep, n=318 sends, the naive sum is USD 0.412 against USD 0.231 after differencing.
+
+The earlier break-even prediction, turn calls cheaper around user message 185-215 and with compactions around message 400 or later for Sonnet and never for Haiku, is superseded as guidance by turns 57 and 82 under this replay's stated unit weights. Those predictions concerned a different script and xhigh effort. The original short-chat turn-call measurements remain valid, with compactions excluded, one run per arm and one-hour write weights:
+
+| round-1 chat | turns per arm | UniiChat-to-continued turn-cost ratio |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| Sonnet | 26 | 1.45 |
+| Haiku | 26 | 1.54 |
+| Haiku, scaled view limits | 42 | 1.84 |
+
+'''
+replace('## limits of the result {#limits}', short_corrections + '## limits of the result {#limits}')
+replace('These are accounting assumptions applied to measured usage, not money paid on the subscription.',
+        'The table recomputes measured usage under these stated weights.')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'To combine Sonnet turns'), '''The gist says Claude Code subscriptions mark with their own one-hour lifetime. Our cache-lifetime probe found that `CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL=5m` gives five-minute writes there. The trial core also hard-coded one hour on its view mark, contrary to the gist's API advice. Thus the observed one-hour columns carry a 2-times write cost that the API design does not ask for.
+
+The table weights Haiku units at 0.05 of Sonnet units while retaining the gist-style 0.1 read weight within each model. That one-twentieth ratio comes from fitting the CLI's cost field to measured usage. The fitted USD prices per million tokens were Sonnet 2 / 4 / 0.10 / 10 and Haiku 0.10 / 0.20 / 0.01 / 0.50, in input / one-hour write / read / output order. They were not checked against a price list or an invoice. The Sonnet fit implies reads at 0.05 times input; Haiku's fit implies 0.1. These fitted accounting rates explain the CLI estimates below.''')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'The checkpoint totals include'),
+        'The checkpoint totals include all compaction sends assigned by call-start time before the next turn-call starts, with the remaining sends assigned to the final turn. This assignment includes background compaction and the resumed run in the same evaluation fold.')
+replace('| units, one-hour writes | 57 | 82 | recomputed from measured usage |',
+        '| units, one-hour writes, Haiku at 1/20 of Sonnet | 57 | 82 | recomputed from measured usage |\n| units, one-hour writes, Haiku at 1/3 of Sonnet | 57 | not reached | price sensitivity on measured usage |')
+replace('| units, five-minute writes |', '| units, five-minute writes, Haiku at 1/20 |')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'The five-minute columns assume'), '''The five-minute row reprices the same token counts and assumes no extra misses from expiry. Warm probes found identical token counts over 24 Haiku and 8 Sonnet turns; only the write class changed. One five-minute entry missed after a 330-second pause, n=1 expiry sample. We did not run the long replay with five-minute entries, and human pauses or long waits could require extra cold writes.''')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'The endpoint is near level'), '''Including compactions, UniiChat remains just above the baseline in CLI USD at the endpoint. The fitted Sonnet read weight of 0.05 benefits a baseline dominated by cache reads, whereas the weighted-token table uses 0.1. At turn 112 the baseline read 32.28 million of 32.62 million input-side tokens from cache, n=112 turns.''')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'At the endpoint, the baseline read'), '')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'The observed savings also depend'), '''Compaction consumed 13.73 million Haiku units over 2,383 sends, 4.7 times the UniiChat arm's 2.91 million Sonnet turn units before price scaling. At the fitted one-twentieth ratio, total weighted cost ends at 3.60 million units against 4.61 million for the baseline. At one third, the same usage costs 7.49 million units, and no total-cost crossover occurs within 112 turns. Cheap compaction is therefore a condition of the reported saving. These two price scenarios do not establish a universal threshold or break-even length.''')
+
+replace('E4 used one fixed synthetic corpus', 'The effort sweep used one fixed synthetic corpus')
+replace('The original report\'s maximum-tries claim was not reconciled by the final audit, so it is not used here.', '')
+replace('In the longer replay, low-effort compaction accounted for about 23% of the UniiChat arm\'s estimated USD, n=2,383 sends. The earlier roughly 5-times to 58-times whole-arm penalties have disappeared in this setup. This is a changed workload and setting, not a repaired estimate of what those earlier xhigh chats cost.',
+        'In the longer replay, low-effort compaction accounted for about 23% of the UniiChat arm\'s estimated USD, n=2,383 sends. The short trial\'s whole-arm penalties, as originally reported, 5 to 58 times; corrected, 5 to about 57, do not describe this setup. The script and effort changed.')
+
+replace(paragraph(post, 'The baseline never encountered'), '''The baseline never encountered the failure that motivated the design. Claude Code reported a 1,000,000-token Sonnet window. Its prompt first passed 200,000 tokens during turn 82 and ended at 344,607 tokens, with no auto-compaction events over 112 turns. We observed neither recall loss nor broken standing orders in the graded checks. A 200,000-token window could have required compaction within this replay; that alternative was not run, and its exact trigger would depend on reserved output and CLI policy. Filling the reported million-token window was estimated to need 300+ turns of this script, an inference beyond the observed endpoint. The failure the gist sets out to fix, a session compacted, dropped or degraded by length, did not occur in this baseline, so the design's main memory benefit was not tested.''')
+replace('The final reviewer accepted the stored grades without rerunning the grader. We checked the source fields for this account; we did not replace that grading method with independent outcome verification.',
+        'The stored grades remain regex and file-inspection results; we cross-checked the source fields without independently regrading outcomes.')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'Three of UniiChat\'s four recall misses'), '''Three of UniiChat's four recall misses came from turn 105, which exhausted the trial harness's eight-request allowance on zooms and produced no answers. The gist sets no zoom limit. Both arms had the same eight-request cap on that turn; the baseline answered in one request. The as-run score is 50/54. Outside that capped turn, UniiChat had one miss in 51 questions, an exclusion analysis that leaves the original result intact. The remaining miss concerned a correction the agent had never applied and later described as on hold. A larger allowance might help retrieval, but we did not test it.''')
+replace('The separate skipped-task row prevents that flattering interpretation.', '')
+replace('E4\'s exact-substring test', 'The sweep\'s exact-substring test')
+
+replace('## failure modes at scale {#failures}\n\n', '''## failure modes at scale {#failures}
+
+The replay compaction findings below concern Haiku at low effort, our choice; the gist reports xhigh. The sweep found no tool calls at xhigh, 0/184 sends, or high, 0/212, against 4/318 at low. We did not run the replay at high or xhigh. Turn replies came from Sonnet at the CLI's default effort, so they also do not measure compactor xhigh behaviour.
+
+''')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'The shared system prompt asks'), '''The shared system prompt asks the same model to handle turns and compactions. On 12 of 112 Sonnet turns the reply used the compaction format under the harness's broad rule, which accepts a message-kind tag followed by a colon or parenthesis. A narrower regex in the source review counted 9/112; that is a source-reported alternative classification. Eleven of the broadly classified turns were orders, facts or corrections to note: 6 orders, 3 facts and 2 corrections. One was an edit task, turn 35, the only overlap with the five edit turns that changed nothing.
+
+Turn 35's reply began with this synthetic example:
+
+> user (msg 323): item_line in report.py breaks columns when a name is longer than 24 characters; cut it to 23 characters plus `~`. Not started; no edits yet, report.py's item_line source not re-read since msg 148.
+
+The appended user-instruction block identified a synthetic maintainer and inventory project, restricted work to its repository, and asked for independent tool calls together. The new message followed the `<chat>` view as plain task text, without a `Compaction:` prefix. Later standing orders arrived through the scripted chat. This prompt and message framing may contribute to mode confusion; no alternative framing was tested. Separately, 5 of 63 add, fix and probe tasks produced no file change.''')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'Compactions also used tools despite'), '''There are two findings about compactor tools. First, the model attempted tool calls despite "Call no tools, and output only the line, without an id+n| head." The sweep listed memory tools; the replay listed those plus the turn's Read, Write, Edit and Bash tools, following the shared-tool design. At low effort, 266/2,383 sends contained a tool call.''')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'A send can contain more than one tool call'), '''A send can contain multiple tool calls; the rate counts sends and the other rows count calls. Second, the Claude Code route executed 159 read-only or no-op shell commands and one file read even though compactions had an empty tool allow list and permission mode `dontAsk`. No compactor write was applied. This execution reflects our route's permission behaviour. The gist says the shared tools are "never called"; a harness that owns tool execution can discard attempted calls instead. That would prevent execution, although the model's attempt would still violate the instruction. The unsuccessful rows include permission refusals and runtime errors.''')
+replace('Sonnet medium wrote the answers 1517 and 5040 on its partial 32-message corpus, which does not contain all five baits.',
+        'Sonnet low wrote 5040, and Sonnet medium wrote 1517 and 5040, on their partial 32-message corpora, which do not contain all five baits.')
+replace('These are measured scheduling observations, not isolated latency benchmarks for a person using the system.',
+        'This is a scheduling estimate from measured call times, n=112, under the trial\'s shared-account schedule.')
+replace('Two turns added 95 and 90 lines. The next calls wrote the whole view again, consistent with exhausting the marked-prefix lookback.',
+        'Two turns added 95 and 90 lines. The next calls wrote the whole view again, consistent with exhausting the gist\'s 20-block lookback with four lines per block.')
+
+replace('E6 measured the smaller remaining blocks separately.', 'The overhead probes measured the smaller remaining blocks separately.')
+for old, new in [('E5 stock arm', 'replay stock arm'), ('E5 thinned arm', 'replay thinned arm'),
+                 ('E6 trivial-message Haiku probes', 'trivial-message Haiku probes')]:
+    replace(old, new)
+replace('stock with the owner\'s normal configuration', 'stock with a normally configured install')
+replace('Stock tools and skills cost 889,826 units against 625,676 for the thinned session.',
+        'Stock tools and skills cost 889,826 units, the thinned continued session 625,676, and UniiChat turn calls 667,964, n=37 turns per arm. UniiChat also ran thinned, and its figure excludes compactions; on turn calls it was already below stock but above the thinned baseline at this checkpoint.')
+replace('the stock arm did not include the owner\'s normal configuration.', 'the stock arm had user settings disabled.')
+replace('Captured request shapes and local SDK source let E6 estimate', 'Captured request shapes and SDK source let the overhead study estimate')
+replace('Placeholder-size accounting remained imperfect, and not every part of the specification was implemented. Message delivery into an active call, images, paged long messages, subagents, zoom by name and device state were outside the exercised implementation.',
+        'Placeholder-size accounting remained imperfect, and the omitted features listed above remain outside the result.')
+replace(paragraph(post, 'The baseline and UniiChat also had different prompts'), '''The baseline request used Read, Write, Edit and Bash, Claude Code's default system prompt with the same synthetic project instructions appended, the accumulated session, and the new message. UniiChat used those file and shell tools plus `zoom` and `date`, the gist's system prompt with the project instructions appended, the saved view and the new message, in a fresh session. The captured Claude Code request shape also places a reminder before the view and an environment block after the new message. Both arms use that CLI wrapper; the 170- and 357-token sizes are measurements from separate probes, n=1 each, rather than measurements on every replay request. Dynamic environment state follows the view, as the gist requests. The extra reminder precedes it, so our input is not exactly the gist's bare request layout.
+
+The subscription supplies usage and CLI estimates rather than an invoice we can validate. Fitted CLI prices and alternative unit weights are accounting assumptions. A smaller window, a different compactor price or effort, and a larger request allowance could change the comparison; none of those long replay alternatives was measured.''')
+replace('The final skeptical report takes precedence where experiment reports disagree. The frozen summary distinguishes recomputed usage, harness grades and paper estimates.',
+        'It distinguishes source-reported results, recomputed usage, harness grades, repricing and paper estimates. This draft is intended for the design author; publication of the unlisted gist material remains outside this work.')
+
+start = post.index('These questions concern the saved specification')
+replace(post[start:], '''These questions concern gaps the saved gist does not answer. Our continued-session comparison is an additional test, not a savings claim attributed to the design.
+
+1. Have you compared total spend or recall against a cached continued session with a 1,000,000-token window, and from what length does UniiChat win? Our baseline passed 200,000 tokens at turn 82 without compacting, so we would also like to understand whether the intended comparison used a smaller window.
+2. Were the simulated 98.6% and 96.2% prefix cache-read shares checked against real usage? Our all-input-side shares were 95.6% for turns and 93.0% for compactions, a different denominator.
+3. Does the advice against one-hour entries assume a chat without long pauses, or have you measured five-minute entries plus cold rewrites for that case? Have you seen the CLI bill Sonnet reads at 0.05 times input, as its cost-field fit implies here?
+4. Is the compactor's "never called" tool rule enforced by your harness or only by the prompt? We saw attempts in 266/2,383 Haiku-low sends, and Claude Code executed some despite the empty allow list.
+5. Have ordinary turns returned compaction-format lines under your shared prompt? Our broad classification found 12/112 Sonnet turns, including one edit task.
+6. How does your implementation handle a turn that adds enough lines to exceed the 20-block cache lookback? After turns adding 95 and 90 lines, our next calls rewrote the whole view.
+''')
